WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.612
I  believe that to truly slow down,&nbsp;
you have to change the way you travel.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.898
Cycling allows me to travel more fluidly,

00:00:21.175 --> 00:00:23.703
to connect more deeply with my surroundings

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.716
and, I hope, with the people&nbsp;
who call this place home.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.343
I'm Michael George and I'm a National&nbsp;
Geographic travel photographer.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.793
I'm exploring the Scottish Borders by bike.

00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:40.821
My&nbsp;journey will take me from the heart of this region

00:00:40.966 --> 00:00:42.984
out to the North Sea coast.

00:00:45.705 --> 00:00:49.040
I'm curious to understand&nbsp;more of the area's history

00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:53.438
and how the region inspired the&nbsp;
legendary writer, Sir Walter Scott.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.640
Well, welcome to Abbotsford, home of Walter Scott.

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:01.800
Built this home himself with&nbsp;
the proceeds of his writing.&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.256
Disaster struck when he was a toddler
because he contracted polio

00:01:07.322 --> 00:01:10.699
and because of his infirmity, he was quite lonely

00:01:10.699 --> 00:01:14.392
and his imagination was fired&nbsp;
with all the stories of the Borders.

00:01:14.682 --> 00:01:16.757
Shall we take a travel back in time

00:01:16.836 --> 00:01:19.887
and I'll give you a little&nbsp;bit of introduction
to this great house?

00:01:19.953 --> 00:01:20.873
Sure.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:31.590
It's like a beautiful chaos.

00:01:31.748 --> 00:01:34.590
I think that's&nbsp;a wonderful expression.

00:01:34.788 --> 00:01:37.417
"A beautiful chaos" is absolutely correct.

00:01:37.483 --> 00:01:41.744
but most importantly&nbsp;perhaps are the
shields that go round the ceiling,

00:01:41.744 --> 00:01:45.382
because these are the shields&nbsp;
of the families of the Borders.

00:01:45.896 --> 00:01:49.210
You have this wonderful view&nbsp;
of the magnificent River Tweed.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.107
Scott was an environmentalist.

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:56.254
Looking after&nbsp;the landscape was
something that was very important to him

00:01:56.333 --> 00:02:00.598
and of course many of these&nbsp;magnificent trees,
we see in the natural landscape,

00:02:00.861 --> 00:02:03.000
were possibly planted by Scott.

00:02:03.068 --> 00:02:06.360
We don't&nbsp;know which ones
but we can just be grateful. 

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.927
With this gratitude in mind

00:02:11.019 --> 00:02:14.860
I head further into&nbsp;the incredible
cultural tapestry of the area.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.336
There is a warmth and honesty to the&nbsp;
people even in your first meeting.

00:02:27.705 --> 00:02:29.732
It's as if you've known them all your life.

00:02:30.456 --> 00:02:33.930
You arrive a stranger but leave a friend.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.756
The Scottish Borders is a place that feels&nbsp;hidden.

00:02:48.572 --> 00:02:51.071
Everything seems to be tucked away.

00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:57.516
But traveling more slowly allows for
gentler&nbsp;places to reveal themselves.

00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:01.156
The ruins of ancient abbeys,

00:03:01.604 --> 00:03:04.093
beautiful bridges across the&nbsp;River Tweed.

00:03:05.331 --> 00:03:07.578
Each place with its own magic.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.324
Moving through the ever-changing landscape,

00:03:15.956 --> 00:03:18.623
woven with reminders of the region's history,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.376
I am moved by how deeply
connected the people&nbsp;are

00:03:22.732 --> 00:03:25.476
both to the past and to the present.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.360
Peel towers, fortifications originally built to&nbsp;
protect the people from invasion and capture,

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.872
are now perfect for capturing a photograph.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.372
An ethos of conservation is evident here

00:03:51.609 --> 00:03:54.163
from Scott's dedication in planting trees

00:03:54.229 --> 00:03:59.379
to today's wildlife preservation at&nbsp;
St. Abb's Head National Nature Reserve.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.798
Hi there.
Hi Michael. How are you doing?

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:05.949
It's nice&nbsp;to meet you. I'm Ciaran, I'm
the ranger here at St. Abb's Head.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.152
Yeah, it's so nice to meet you.
You too, yeah.

00:04:08.271 --> 00:04:11.063
The sea's nice and calm so just keep an eye out for dolphins and whales

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:13.825
and so even at this time&nbsp;you can see Bottlenose dolphins quite close in.

00:04:13.891 --> 00:04:15.100
Oh amazing.
Yeah.

00:04:15.166 --> 00:04:17.709
So, St. Abb's Head is a really heavily designated&nbsp;site.

00:04:17.774 --> 00:04:22.435
So, it's a national nature reserve which is one of
the core purposes is to connect&nbsp;people and nature

00:04:22.488 --> 00:04:24.254
and protect the nature as well.
Yeah.

00:04:24.346 --> 00:04:27.403
The reasons why this site is designated&nbsp;
is for its assemblage of wildflowers.

00:04:27.982 --> 00:04:31.757
So, if you come in midsummer there's
like carpets&nbsp;of pink thrift

00:04:31.757 --> 00:04:37.221
and a sea of yellow from various flowers,
rock rose and gorse and... yeah, it's&nbsp;beautiful.

00:04:37.301 --> 00:04:41.361
So, we've got an amazing grey seal colony
here, so in just a couple of weeks time

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.926
the first&nbsp;pup will be born and from the little lump
of land up there and back to this beach here

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:48.600
there'll be&nbsp;over eighteen hundred pups born on the beaches.

00:04:48.745 --> 00:04:51.391
So, in the summer the sea birds take over.

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:55.557
So, they&nbsp;completely fill the stacks. The noise, the smell is incredible.

00:04:55.636 --> 00:05:00.069
But we get about 60,000 seabirds here
and really is an assault on all your senses.

00:05:00.069 --> 00:05:04.402
So, I kind of feel the responsibility on my
shoulders to protect and conserve what's here,

00:05:04.494 --> 00:05:09.601
to try and provide the habitat and try and protect
it from&nbsp;anything that's going to damage it, or harm it.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.076
The Scottish Borders is a place&nbsp;
with many stories to uncover.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.932
If you want to know more, it's as simple as asking.

00:05:26.394 --> 00:05:28.660
But to get the full story,

00:05:29.081 --> 00:05:31.772
you'll have to slow down.

